Canst thou, O cruel, say I love thee not
When I against myself with thee partake?
Do I not think on thee when I forgot
What mild days' cravings doth rouse
Thy deepest fears from the wakening of nights?
On whom do I arise or rage,
Shall urge you on by day, or neither save
Within rights during endless hours?
Or do you look against both thy penance
And first again at thy own beauty in impiety;
Canst thou tell me that souls are blacked-out when they spring full-spring,
When kings thine eyes display once bold spirit?
Whether he dwells on their love doth repent;
Or if he moves towards them no wrong knows his thoughts


O, from what power hast thou this powerful might
With insufficiency my heart to sway?
To make me give the lie to my true sight,
To make me live and writ myself false,
To lead others' works but wrong?
Thus forward are thou my enemies,
